WEBVTT

00:04.240 --> 00:08.280
Quick test: can you sing a major scale from the bottom to top and right back down?

00:09.040 --> 00:12.120
[music plays]

00:16.080 --> 00:21.960
Good job! Now here's the thing: almost no song you actually love uses a scale that plainly.

00:22.800 --> 00:25.560
This week, you're learning to build major scales and use key signatures,

00:26.160 --> 00:31.480
but scales are just collections of notes, typically not patterns in which a real melody moves.

00:32.160 --> 00:39.760
There are a few exceptions to this, the most famous being "Joy to the World," which has a descending major scale that starts it right off the bat.

00:40.640 --> 00:44.520
[music plays]

00:45.320 --> 00:55.560
And in ascending major scales found in Alicia Keys' "Un-Breakable."

00:58.280 --> 01:00.640
But these are unusual in the greater musical world.

01:01.480 --> 01:07.720
Scales hide inside famous songs all the time, but rarely as a tidy climb up or down from DO to DO.

01:08.280 --> 01:16.000
Some songs sit in a totally different part of the scale's range, and most don't even need consecutive notes or a complete set of notes to do their job.

01:16.640 --> 01:21.040
For instance, Katy Perry's "Firework" provides a mostly complete scale in its pre-chorus,

01:21.880 --> 01:27.190
but it moves from MI up more than an octave up to SOL instead of DO to DO.

01:27.790 --> 01:34.790
[music plays]

01:45.550 --> 01:49.350
But as I said, scales are usually just collections of notes,

01:49.750 --> 01:54.270
so you perceive them as defining a key even when they aren't just stepping up or down.

01:54.750 --> 01:59.830
Your mind automatically gathers which notes are being used and figures out which key they belong to.

02:00.190 --> 02:04.990
And if there are extra notes outside of the scale, your mind filters them as being extra —

02:05.470 --> 02:09.950
or, as we call them, "chromatic" — rather than as essential parts of the key.

02:10.910 --> 02:15.870
For instance, Justin Bieber's song "Stay" has an incredibly jagged melody;

02:16.670 --> 02:21.230
in music theory, we call this "disjunct motion." But when the notes are massed together as a group,

02:21.790 --> 02:25.990
it turns out that they perfectly match five notes of the D-flat major scale.

02:30.230 --> 02:35.510
This diagram shows you how every one of those melody pitches goes with one of the scale degrees.

02:36.990 --> 02:40.270
And what about the other two notes? To make the full seven of a scale?

02:41.070 --> 02:45.670
We don't really need all seven pitches to determine a key, but we DO get them anyway.

02:46.230 --> 02:49.630
We get them from the chords that run underneath the sung melody.
